# Telecom Equipment Failure Analysis

Exploratory analysis of industrial machine failure patterns, using the AI4I predictive-maintenance dataset (10,000 machine records, sensor readings including air/process temperature, rotational speed, torque, and tool wear).

> **Note:** like the churn project, this is EDA and feature engineering, not a trained predictive model — no classifier is built here.

## Why this project

Machine failure in this dataset is rare (339 failures out of 10,000 records, 3.4%) — a realistic and difficult class imbalance for real industrial maintenance data. The goal here was to find which sensor readings actually separate failing machines from healthy ones before attempting to predict failure.

## What it does

1. **Data check**: confirmed no missing values across all sensor columns; dropped identifier columns (`UDI`, `Product ID`) that carry no predictive signal.
2. **Failure rate**: 3.39% of machines failed (339/10,000) — a meaningfully imbalanced target.
3. **Feature engineering**:
   - `temperature difference` = process temperature − air temperature
   - `power` = torque × rotational speed
   - `high_wear_risk` = binary flag for tool wear under 200 minutes
   - `tool_wear_group` = binned into Low/Medium/High
4. **Tool wear finding**: machines in the "High" tool-wear group failed at 7.4%, more than 3x the rate of the Low (2.2%) and Medium (2.3%) groups — the clearest single risk signal found.
5. **Cleaned output** saved for future modeling.

## Key finding

Tool wear is the standout risk factor — failure rate roughly triples once a machine crosses into the high-wear band, while temperature and torque showed less clear-cut separation on their own (though the engineered `power` feature may capture their combined effect better than either alone).

## What I'd do next

- Train a classifier (Random Forest + SMOTE, same pattern used successfully in the Credit Risk Scorer and Diabetes Predictor projects) — with only 3.4% positive cases, this needs the same imbalance handling.
- Given the tool-wear finding, I'd specifically check whether `tool_wear_group` and `power` together are strong enough predictors on their own before adding the full sensor set — simpler models are easier to justify for an actual maintenance-scheduling use case.

## Files

- `Telecom equipment failure.ipynb` — full EDA and feature engineering
- `data.csv` — source data
- `maintenance_cleaned.csv` — cleaned, feature-engineered output (generated by the notebook)

## Stack

Python, pandas, matplotlib, seaborn
